<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Divya Upadesh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1775</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Divya Upadesh (&quot;Divine Counsel&quot;) is the set of final political teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a>, the founder of unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, delivered to his courtiers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuwakot">Nuwakot</a> shortly before his death in 1775 and recorded by them. It describes Nepal as &quot;a yam between two boulders&quot;, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, urges caution towards both, and gives advice on defence, the army, trade and the encouragement of home industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/divya-upadesh.jpg" alt="Divya Upadesh" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yogi Naraharinath · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Divyopadesh_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
